#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"}] :question/id #uuid "5ebf00aa-406d-5286-b4ae-0f0044ce9b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21905" :question/problem "Evaluate the indicator function\n$\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7]$." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is true, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is false$.\n\nHere, the predicate is $9 > 7$. Since $9$ is greater than $7$, the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7] = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1900018f-1bac-5623-b45f-e3d9c89f5d74" :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "1687d5ff-6b48-52d9-a947-3425227af366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21906" :question/problem "Consider the function\n$f(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] \\cdot x^2 + \\mathbf{1}[x < 1] \\cdot (-x)$.\nWhat is $f(-2)$?" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is true, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is false$.\n\nFor $x = -2$, we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 \\geq 1] = 0$ (since $-2 \\geq 1$ is false)\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x < 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 < 1] = 1$ (since $-2 < 1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 1[- 2 \\ge 1] \\cdot (-2)^{2} + 1[- 2 < 1] \\cdot (- (-2)) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 4 + 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x = -2 < 1$, the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e434dbd-b33c-5997-b3ba-043233cfbc89" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Indicator Notations"}] :question/id #uuid "c831e4d2-736d-5ee1-9c0e-55cee535f361" :question/math-academy-id "e-22020" :question/problem "Let $H = \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}$. Which of the following is equivalent to $\\mathbf{1}_H(0)$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n2. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\notin \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n3. $\\mathbf{1}[H \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n4. $\\mathbf{1}[\\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\} \\in 0]$\n5. $\\mathbf{1}[0 = H]$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is true, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]P[/math] is false$.\n\nRecall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_H(x)$ of a set $H$ equals $1$ if $x \\in H$ and $0$ if $x \\notin H$. This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in H]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(0) = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in H] = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]\n$$"}]}
